[SPEAKER_09]: I think that's all the administrative type announcements and updates.

237
00:18:41,567 --> 00:18:42,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, thank you.

238
00:18:44,077 --> 00:18:48,584
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to get closer to scheduling a summer meeting.

239
00:18:48,664 --> 00:18:55,395
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, we were thinking that maybe the mayor could attend the summer meeting, but you probably don't know her schedule, do you?

240
00:18:55,536 --> 00:18:56,437
[SPEAKER_01]: At least you'll have to ask.

241
00:18:57,773 --> 00:19:08,841
[SPEAKER_09]: It would be, she may be able to do the summer meeting because the difficulty is that the school committee meets the first and third Mondays of every month and we meet the first Mondays.

242
00:19:09,521 --> 00:19:13,424
[SPEAKER_09]: And so this conflicts directly in this meeting.

243
00:19:13,464 --> 00:19:17,407
[SPEAKER_09]: The other alternative is that this meeting used to start at six.

244
00:19:18,148 --> 00:19:24,913
[SPEAKER_09]: And sometimes we would have the mayor come for like 15 or 20 minutes at the very beginning of our meeting before she went to school committee.

245
00:19:26,394 --> 00:19:28,875
[SPEAKER_09]: And that I think would be something that she would be open to.

246
00:19:28,895 --> 00:19:41,285
[SPEAKER_09]: So I also put that out there as an alternative option that I haven't run by her, but as long as they don't have a committee of the whole for them before their meeting.

247
00:19:43,246 --> 00:19:46,869
[SPEAKER_01]: These meetings happen during the summer for school committee.

248
00:19:47,129 --> 00:19:48,950
[SPEAKER_09]: School committee will stop during the summer.

249
00:19:48,970 --> 00:19:53,794
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so she's gonna be available unless she's away.

250
00:19:54,993 --> 00:19:55,213
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

251
00:19:55,713 --> 00:19:58,175
[SPEAKER_01]: So maybe you could ask her.

252
00:20:00,016 --> 00:20:02,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Now we want you to be at the meeting too, if you can.

253
00:20:02,557 --> 00:20:09,581
[SPEAKER_01]: So we're negotiating between mid-July and mid-August, those weeks.

254
00:20:10,641 --> 00:20:14,823
[SPEAKER_09]: Oh, we were talking about doing this in person, right?

255
00:20:15,004 --> 00:20:15,864
[SPEAKER_01]: In person, yes.

256
00:20:15,884 --> 00:20:17,925
[SPEAKER_09]: That was also the problem with me.

257
00:20:18,045 --> 00:20:23,148
[SPEAKER_09]: I can't do the meeting if I'm at the Jersey Shore, whether I happily Zoom in or not.

258
00:20:23,700 --> 00:20:24,701
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, right, right, right.

259
00:20:24,721 --> 00:20:27,924
[SPEAKER_01]: We want to actually be able to see each other.

260
00:20:27,964 --> 00:20:35,810
[SPEAKER_01]: So yeah, let me know, everybody on the committee, yours, I sent you an email.

261
00:20:35,830 --> 00:20:42,335
[SPEAKER_01]: It was sort of buried in the email, but it's basically starting the week of July 17th.

262
00:20:45,358 --> 00:20:50,842
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the next three weeks after that, I'm giving a window, pick one of those four.

263
00:20:51,042 --> 00:20:52,443
[SPEAKER_01]: And it doesn't have to be Monday either.

264
00:20:52,463 --> 00:20:52,824
[SPEAKER_01]: It could be,

265
00:20:54,181 --> 00:21:00,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Tuesday, Wednesday, I wouldn't do it Friday night, but I would consider, you know, Tuesday, Wednesday or Thursday night.

266
00:21:01,346 --> 00:21:20,919
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but we can try Monday night as a first option, but just let me know your availability during that period of time, those four weeks starting on July 17th and Monday through Thursday, and we'll try to pick the one, but I guess key things would be, it would be nice if the mayor could come.

267
00:21:22,109 --> 00:21:23,549
[SPEAKER_01]: And it would be nice if Alicia could come.

268
00:21:23,589 --> 00:21:24,190
[SPEAKER_01]: So let me know.

269
00:21:25,010 --> 00:21:25,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

270
00:21:27,190 --> 00:21:29,591
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm away the week of July 18th is the Monday, right?

271
00:21:29,611 --> 00:21:31,011
[SPEAKER_09]: No, the week of the July 25th.

272
00:21:31,031 --> 00:21:35,193
[SPEAKER_09]: I'm away the week of July 25th.

273
00:21:35,213 --> 00:21:42,414
[SPEAKER_09]: And I might be away through like Wednesday, the week after that.

274
00:21:54,106 --> 00:21:54,466
[SPEAKER_01]: Noted.

275
00:21:54,487 --> 00:22:14,908
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just gather everyone's information and try to, but if you could Alicia, if you could ask the mayor for that same period of time, if she's available just to come over to our backyard and she knows where it is, of course, she's been there before.

276
00:22:18,866 --> 00:22:19,066
[SPEAKER_09]: Okay.

277
00:22:19,086 --> 00:22:24,148
[SPEAKER_09]: Just to be clear, we will have to, even though it's in your yard, we would still have to post it.

278
00:22:24,848 --> 00:22:26,709
[SPEAKER_01]: And it would have to be a public meeting.

279
00:22:26,769 --> 00:22:27,269
[SPEAKER_01]: That's right.

280
00:22:27,289 --> 00:22:29,630
[SPEAKER_09]: I just want to be clear.

281
00:22:30,230 --> 00:22:41,455
[SPEAKER_01]: People can bring their family members, there's things to do, sandbox, river, boating, whatever, that type of thing.

282
00:22:41,475 --> 00:22:46,456
[SPEAKER_05]: Hey, Bob, I sent you a message through the chat.

283
00:22:46,476 --> 00:22:47,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm sorry.

284
00:22:48,350 --> 00:22:58,854
[SPEAKER_05]: I sent you a message through the chat for a question, if you could review the open meeting laws.

285
00:22:58,914 --> 00:22:59,514
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, I will.

286
00:22:59,614 --> 00:23:11,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, let's see, there was a question, I think we decided, I know that I'm aware that if you have less than a quorum, you could have emails discussing things.

287
00:23:13,150 --> 00:23:15,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Now we've defined quorum is now seven.

288
00:23:15,031 --> 00:23:22,416
[SPEAKER_01]: So we have to have, it has to be up to six people can talk about anything they want, I guess, pretty much via email.

289
00:23:23,516 --> 00:23:34,603
[SPEAKER_01]: I know we can't make decisions, you know, certainly can't make decisions outside a meeting, but you can have discussions, heated discussions or whatever.

290
00:23:35,364 --> 00:23:35,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

291
00:23:36,444 --> 00:23:40,907
[SPEAKER_05]: Um, so this is a good point then to me, for me to understand.

292
00:23:41,236 --> 00:23:45,499
[SPEAKER_05]: So if you invite us and we have a meeting at someone's house, we have to post it.

293
00:23:48,281 --> 00:23:58,568
[SPEAKER_09]: If it is a meeting of this committee and it is a public meeting, then therefore it is a public meeting and he can, and it has to be posted and open to the public.

294
00:24:00,089 --> 00:24:02,931
[SPEAKER_05]: But if it's a private meeting, what about a subcommittee meeting?

295
00:24:03,512 --> 00:24:07,875
[SPEAKER_09]: A subcommittee meeting would not, if it's less in quorum, you wouldn't need to post it.

296
00:24:08,035 --> 00:24:10,917
[SPEAKER_09]: And Barry has a comment on that.

297
00:24:11,953 --> 00:24:22,958
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, a subcommittee of the Human Rights Commission discovered otherwise, according to materials written by the Attorney General.

298
00:24:23,778 --> 00:24:33,742
[SPEAKER_03]: An officially designated subcommittee of an official committee is itself an official public body.

299
00:24:35,003 --> 00:24:40,645
[SPEAKER_03]: And so if you have a subcommittee of three members,

300
00:24:41,987 --> 00:24:50,632
[SPEAKER_03]: It is not a quorum of the whole committee, but two of those three members would constitute a quorum of the subcommittee.

301
00:24:50,752 --> 00:24:57,095
[SPEAKER_03]: Those meetings have to be posted and those discussions are subject to open meeting law.

302
00:24:58,476 --> 00:25:08,961
[SPEAKER_03]: You can have informal work groups, I believe, but once you create an actual subcommittee, you have created another public body.

303
00:25:11,800 --> 00:25:12,020
[SPEAKER_09]: know.

304
00:25:12,040 --> 00:25:14,421
[SPEAKER_03]: Or maybe it's bad to know.

305
00:25:14,521 --> 00:25:17,621
[SPEAKER_03]: Maybe I just should have kept my mouth shut and we could have.

306
00:25:17,681 --> 00:25:22,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Matthew had a question on chat.

307
00:25:22,062 --> 00:25:26,463
[SPEAKER_01]: He said that what can you do below a quorum?

308
00:25:26,483 --> 00:25:28,823
[SPEAKER_01]: I think you can discuss things.

309
00:25:28,883 --> 00:25:31,624
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't vote on anything, but I think you can discuss.

310
00:25:34,104 --> 00:25:35,705
[SPEAKER_05]: I still find this a little confusing.

311
00:25:35,985 --> 00:25:36,225
[SPEAKER_05]: So I

312
00:25:38,014 --> 00:25:51,201
[SPEAKER_05]: If you invite the energy and environment committee to your house, me or you or anybody, that's considered a public meeting, even though we might not be talking about energy and environment related, you know, agenda items.

313
00:25:51,942 --> 00:25:52,782
[SPEAKER_09]: Not in this case.

314
00:25:52,862 --> 00:25:56,144
[SPEAKER_09]: He's actually suggesting that he would have the meeting.

315
00:25:56,164 --> 00:25:58,025
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be an official meeting.

316
00:25:58,685 --> 00:25:59,646
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, I didn't realize that.

317
00:25:59,846 --> 00:26:01,467
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay.

318
00:26:01,647 --> 00:26:03,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Our summer meeting will be in person.

319
00:26:05,529 --> 00:26:05,789
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay.

320
00:26:07,252 --> 00:26:15,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Since we don't even know, because July 15th is the current expiration of the remote meeting law, we don't know if it's going to be renewed.

321
00:26:15,721 --> 00:26:20,926
[SPEAKER_01]: So we're going to make sure we're adhering to whatever law it is by having it in person.

322
00:26:21,707 --> 00:26:22,027
[SPEAKER_05]: I see.

323
00:26:22,427 --> 00:26:22,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

324
00:26:23,789 --> 00:26:26,912
[SPEAKER_01]: And who knows after that, we might have to meet in person again.

325
00:26:28,005 --> 00:26:37,390
[SPEAKER_09]: I will tell you that I did reach out to all the members of our legislative delegation about the desire to continue to have remote and hybrid meetings.

326
00:26:38,070 --> 00:26:41,412
[SPEAKER_09]: And they are in favor of it.

327
00:26:41,792 --> 00:26:43,333
[SPEAKER_09]: They all wrote back to me.

328
00:26:43,453 --> 00:26:57,120
[SPEAKER_09]: And in fact, actually, Paul Donato said he was working with the state's legal counsel to draft something to allow, at the very least, hybrid remote meetings, members to participate remotely.

329
00:26:58,736 --> 00:27:00,477
[SPEAKER_09]: if not fully remote meetings.

330
00:27:00,997 --> 00:27:12,586
[SPEAKER_09]: And actually gave me the example himself that the, our state, the legislature, the house had an in-person meeting and then six members of the house got COVID right afterwards.

331
00:27:13,646 --> 00:27:19,551
[SPEAKER_09]: So they are kind of inclined to pass this allowance to continue remote meetings.

332
00:27:21,052 --> 00:27:21,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Sounds good.

333
00:27:23,494 --> 00:27:26,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Just one more before we get into the circle of square,

334
00:27:27,273 --> 00:27:31,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Any updates on the Harvester Energy Festival, Alicia?

335
00:27:32,294 --> 00:27:47,277
[SPEAKER_09]: I had asked Loretta, Loretta, sorry, Teresa to book us a shredding truck and an electronics recycling truck so that those would be taken care of up front and confirm the dates with the school and the mayor.

336
00:27:49,157 --> 00:27:50,617
[SPEAKER_09]: That's basically the update.

337
00:27:51,017 --> 00:27:53,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, third Saturday of October.

338
00:27:53,138 --> 00:27:53,378
[SPEAKER_09]: Yeah.

339
00:27:54,018 --> 00:27:54,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

340
00:27:55,897 --> 00:27:57,358
[SPEAKER_09]: and Gaston raised his hand.

341
00:27:59,338 --> 00:28:01,899
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, so just the last comment on the open meeting law.

342
00:28:02,500 --> 00:28:16,886
[SPEAKER_04]: So should someone in the committee, so I just did a quick search and then there are even like webinars that you can do from Massachusetts and this kind of the same as we would do related to the conflict of interest.

343
00:28:19,266 --> 00:28:25,269
[SPEAKER_04]: So should at least one person go through one of this and then be the point of contact

344
00:28:27,486 --> 00:28:33,250
[SPEAKER_04]: relating to open meeting though, or is that a bad idea?

345
00:28:34,651 --> 00:28:50,902
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm just trying to make sure that we are efficient, but at the same time that we don't do anything that we shouldn't be doing because we didn't know about it.

346
00:28:52,549 --> 00:28:52,949
[SPEAKER_08]: Agreed.

347
00:28:53,489 --> 00:29:10,033
[SPEAKER_08]: I would just add, when we started, or when I started, I recall that a number of us, if not the whole committee, went through a number of trainings, including open meeting law and conflict of interest.

348
00:29:11,453 --> 00:29:17,695
[SPEAKER_08]: And I am still not clear if open meeting law has changed significantly since then.

349
00:29:19,175 --> 00:29:39,325
[SPEAKER_08]: But it has been and still kind of continues to be my assumption that discussions, as Matthew has pointed out in his comments, discussions related to our committee needs to be kind of

350
00:29:43,100 --> 00:29:47,384
[SPEAKER_08]: offline or needs to be between a couple of people is not a big deal.

351
00:29:47,624 --> 00:30:01,856
[SPEAKER_08]: But as long as once we put a group together of any size, we can can't be discussing the workings of the committee without posting it as an actual meeting.

352
00:30:04,498 --> 00:30:05,459
[SPEAKER_08]: Anyway, but I agree.

353
00:30:05,579 --> 00:30:09,342
[SPEAKER_08]: I think somebody should kind of check and make sure that

354
00:30:10,202 --> 00:30:14,844
[SPEAKER_08]: we are current on the open meeting law.

355
00:30:17,905 --> 00:30:24,889
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, this Bob, I did do some, I went through a webinar, it may have been last year.

356
00:30:25,049 --> 00:30:36,914
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know if I vaguely, I thought it was pretty clear that if you had less than a quorum, you had a lot more discretion to have a discussion than if you had at least a quorum.

357
00:30:37,494 --> 00:30:38,855
[SPEAKER_01]: But I guess we should check on that.

358
00:30:39,911 --> 00:30:49,503
[SPEAKER_09]: I want to really point out that a lot of this is around bodies that pass laws, issue permits, hold public hearings.

359
00:30:50,304 --> 00:30:54,509
[SPEAKER_09]: Those things are very serious and should be done in daylight.

360
00:30:55,290 --> 00:31:10,557
[SPEAKER_09]: When you're just debating the merits of solar panels and trees, and you want to have that debate among a couple of people before you come back to the committee, that's sort of like on a whole different level, right?

361
00:31:10,737 --> 00:31:15,740
[SPEAKER_09]: It's like having an intellectual discussion about whether or not we want to do this or do that.

362
00:31:16,140 --> 00:31:22,783
[SPEAKER_03]: Alicia, I completely agree with your point, except that's not the way the law is written.

363
00:31:24,303 --> 00:31:30,347
[SPEAKER_03]: The law clearly includes volunteer committees of public bodies.

364
00:31:31,848 --> 00:31:35,671
[SPEAKER_03]: And, you know, you're right, that's the way it should be.

365
00:31:35,851 --> 00:31:44,938
[SPEAKER_03]: It's interesting that the legislature created this law that encumbers us, but does not include themselves.

366
00:31:45,718 --> 00:31:45,899
[SPEAKER_03]: But

367
00:31:50,307 --> 00:31:51,307
[SPEAKER_03]: That's still the law.

368
00:31:51,367 --> 00:32:01,329
[SPEAKER_09]: It's a tendency because the people who write the laws are not familiar with the actual details of the work and don't understand all the consequences of the details.

369
00:32:02,149 --> 00:32:06,130
[SPEAKER_09]: Like they don't always understand all the language they're using or what all the case studies are.

370
00:32:06,930 --> 00:32:08,390
[SPEAKER_09]: We run into it all the time.

371
00:32:08,410 --> 00:32:12,531
[SPEAKER_04]: So I volunteer.

372
00:32:12,591 --> 00:32:19,252
[SPEAKER_04]: So given that when I joined this committee, I was completely perplexed that I couldn't discuss things by email.

373
00:32:20,337 --> 00:32:32,771
[SPEAKER_04]: I volunteer to take a look at this and then either push out an email with all the information or talk briefly about it and then we can go on from there.

374
00:32:32,791 --> 00:32:39,439
[SPEAKER_04]: So I'll go through a training, I'll take a look at all the official documents and then we can see what to do.

375
00:32:40,403 --> 00:32:48,250
[SPEAKER_09]: So part of the difficulty is that people will say, well, only a lawyer should interpret things.

376
00:32:48,730 --> 00:32:53,534
[SPEAKER_09]: On the other hand, if you ask a lawyer, they'll say, well, watch it, read the law yourself, watch the webinar.

377
00:32:53,934 --> 00:32:57,757
[SPEAKER_09]: Well, what if I then write something up for my committee members?

378
00:32:57,797 --> 00:33:01,661
[SPEAKER_09]: Well, you shouldn't do that, Alicia, because you're not a lawyer, right?

379
00:33:03,182 --> 00:33:09,127
[SPEAKER_09]: But you can't expect the lawyer to come to every board and committee and answer every single detailed question we have.

380
00:33:09,664 --> 00:33:11,865
[SPEAKER_09]: that would not be a great use of our resources.

381
00:33:12,846 --> 00:33:14,446
[SPEAKER_09]: It's a big, big catch 22.

382
00:33:14,506 --> 00:33:25,371
[SPEAKER_09]: So, but we did, we can circulate again, the open meeting law training and stuff that they have through the attorney general and all.

383
00:33:28,333 --> 00:33:36,917
[SPEAKER_01]: I was under the impression you could dispense, like I dispense information on the meeting agenda and informational things without,

384
00:33:38,503 --> 00:33:40,784
[SPEAKER_09]: Pushing information out is fine.

385
00:33:41,124 --> 00:33:42,084
[SPEAKER_09]: That is absolutely.

386
00:33:42,944 --> 00:33:46,245
[SPEAKER_09]: Materials get shared to members of the city council, the school community.

387
00:33:46,745 --> 00:33:50,206
[SPEAKER_09]: Sharing materials to people is 100% fine.

388
00:33:50,686 --> 00:33:54,067
[SPEAKER_09]: The difference is when you then debate and discuss the materials.

389
00:33:54,627 --> 00:34:00,449
[SPEAKER_09]: So like a lot of our boards and commissions, somebody will BCC everybody

390
00:34:01,049 --> 00:34:05,050
[SPEAKER_09]: so that you don't accidentally reply all.

391
00:34:05,430 --> 00:34:09,312
[SPEAKER_09]: Replying to one person is not a violation.

392
00:34:09,572 --> 00:34:12,973
[SPEAKER_09]: Sharing a document to the committee is fine.

393
00:34:13,693 --> 00:34:15,914
[SPEAKER_09]: It's the discussion of it that is the problem.

394
00:34:15,934 --> 00:34:17,054
[SPEAKER_05]: Right.

