547
00:51:36,552 --> 00:51:45,218
[SPEAKER_05]: We used to print like three and a page and we cut them and have them there to give people, to inform them about the festival coming up.

548
00:51:46,457 --> 00:51:54,582
[SPEAKER_01]: or QR code, something that's provides information about that, that would be good.

549
00:51:54,903 --> 00:51:55,623
[SPEAKER_04]: I'll make a note.

550
00:51:56,083 --> 00:51:56,444
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay.

551
00:51:57,244 --> 00:52:05,269
[SPEAKER_04]: So related to transportation, Alicia sent out a link related to bus rerouting.

552
00:52:05,289 --> 00:52:14,335
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know where it would be too late for people to give their insight by June 18th, but it could be a great opportunity to let the public know about it.

553
00:52:15,210 --> 00:52:17,131
[SPEAKER_04]: so they can have the input.

554
00:52:20,113 --> 00:52:24,115
[SPEAKER_09]: The public MBTA presentation is on May 19th.

555
00:52:26,357 --> 00:52:29,739
[SPEAKER_09]: I can put it in the chat and I can put it in the minutes.

556
00:52:29,819 --> 00:52:32,981
[SPEAKER_09]: It'll take me a little bit to find that.

557
00:52:33,921 --> 00:52:41,626
[SPEAKER_09]: But I strongly recommend that people who even might take the bus, if the bus was convenient,

558
00:52:43,927 --> 00:52:47,008
[SPEAKER_09]: see the presentation or see the materials afterwards.

559
00:52:47,888 --> 00:52:57,071
[SPEAKER_09]: And if you do take public transportation, you definitely want to weigh in because they are proposing significant changes to the bus system in the greater Boston area.

560
00:52:58,832 --> 00:53:03,593
[SPEAKER_09]: There are a lot of good changes, but there are certainly people who will not be happy with some of them, so.

561
00:53:08,886 --> 00:53:18,250
[SPEAKER_09]: But if you're saying to post that stuff at June 19 at Circle the Square as well, it'll still be there taking comments for at least three months.

562
00:53:18,891 --> 00:53:19,991
[SPEAKER_09]: So it'll still be available.

563
00:53:20,351 --> 00:53:21,672
[SPEAKER_09]: I think that was your point, sorry.

564
00:53:21,732 --> 00:53:23,593
[SPEAKER_09]: I was trying to take notes at the same time.

565
00:53:23,613 --> 00:53:29,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Alicia, for taking notes.

566
00:53:30,957 --> 00:53:35,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's move on to any other discussion on the Circle the Square event.

567
00:53:37,748 --> 00:53:46,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I want to move on, since we know that Dan Koretz, who's joined, is a time limitation.

568
00:53:46,496 --> 00:53:56,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Now Paul Tesliby went to a meeting last week about this issue with leaf blowers.

569
00:53:56,084 --> 00:53:57,786
[SPEAKER_01]: We've talked about leaf blowers before.

570
00:54:00,523 --> 00:54:20,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Before we get Dan to join, I just want to make sure we summarize what was this, if anyone was at the meeting, I don't know, Felicia, you know, at the meeting last week or the meetings, meetings of the whole or meeting subcommittee meetings for the city council.

571
00:54:22,121 --> 00:54:23,682
[SPEAKER_09]: I was not able to attend that one.

572
00:54:27,047 --> 00:54:30,849
[SPEAKER_01]: So I'd hope Paul would be present to do that, but I guess he couldn't make it.

573
00:54:31,510 --> 00:54:40,676
[SPEAKER_01]: But I believe the issue is there's concern about noise and air pollution from gas powered leaf blowers.

574
00:54:40,696 --> 00:54:43,697
[SPEAKER_09]: Is that what our guest is here for?

575
00:54:44,578 --> 00:54:46,399
[SPEAKER_09]: Bob, I don't know what you see on your screen.

576
00:54:46,719 --> 00:54:48,080
[SPEAKER_09]: Dan Kurtz joined us.

577
00:54:48,460 --> 00:54:50,322
[SPEAKER_09]: Is that the topic that he joined us for?

578
00:54:50,342 --> 00:54:51,702
[SPEAKER_01]: That's correct.

579
00:54:52,003 --> 00:54:52,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that okay?

580
00:54:54,226 --> 00:54:54,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

581
00:54:55,127 --> 00:55:04,616
[SPEAKER_01]: He is, he's from Lexington and he Lexington adopted, um, uh, I don't know if it's an ordinance or it was a town meeting law.

582
00:55:05,516 --> 00:55:10,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe Dan, you can go ahead and introduce yourself and explain what Lexington has done.

583
00:55:10,781 --> 00:55:10,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

584
00:55:11,001 --> 00:55:19,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Uh, I am the chair of the, uh, of a committee that advises our, excuse me, our select board on noise issues and, um,

585
00:55:21,623 --> 00:55:34,931
[SPEAKER_00]: After about three and a half years of discussion, we brought forward an article to town meeting this past fall, part of which was fairly conventional, part of which was not.

586
00:55:35,131 --> 00:55:49,040
[SPEAKER_00]: There are a number of towns in this area, Newton, Cambridge, Brookline, Lincoln, some others, that have already put in place some form of restriction on the use of gasoline blowers because of the noise and the

587
00:55:50,524 --> 00:55:54,065
[SPEAKER_00]: the really remarkable level of emissions and the toxicity of emissions.

588
00:55:55,165 --> 00:56:01,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Most of those, the most common thing is to simply ban them in the summer, where there's really no reason for them.

589
00:56:01,366 --> 00:56:09,068
[SPEAKER_00]: What's called in the trade, mow and blow, where people walk behind lawnmowers and blow off the grass clippings.

590
00:56:11,168 --> 00:56:12,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Some have other restrictions.

591
00:56:12,969 --> 00:56:19,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Arlington, for example, limits the number that can be used on a lot at any one time, requires a certain amount of time off out of every hour

592
00:56:20,822 --> 00:56:24,304
[SPEAKER_00]: But the standard thing in the area has been seasonal limitations.

593
00:56:25,985 --> 00:56:49,518
[SPEAKER_00]: We decided to try for the whole ball of wax, and we included in ours a three and a half year phase out, which is common outside of this region, but it's not common, but there are quite a number of cities, for instance, in California that have done this, Washington DC did it, passed it three years ago, but nobody had done it in New England.

594
00:56:51,430 --> 00:57:01,015
[SPEAKER_00]: So we included a three and a half year phase out at the end of which there could be no commercial use of backpack blowers at all.

595
00:57:01,535 --> 00:57:11,200
[SPEAKER_00]: The sole exception was the large wheeled blowers, which you sometimes see on large properties, which are extraordinarily noisy, but they're four stroke engines.

596
00:57:12,320 --> 00:57:13,141
[SPEAKER_00]: So they're much cleaner.

597
00:57:14,041 --> 00:57:17,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Our bylaw allows those only on lots of an acre or more.

598
00:57:18,437 --> 00:57:21,419
[SPEAKER_00]: it gives residents an extra year to phase out the use of theirs.

599
00:57:23,420 --> 00:57:25,801
[SPEAKER_00]: Town meeting, flood court did not support it.

600
00:57:26,461 --> 00:57:30,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Town meeting passed it by an overwhelming margin, 85% in favor.

601
00:57:32,124 --> 00:57:38,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Within 12 hours, landscapers had filed a petition for a referendum to try to overturn the bylaw.

602
00:57:40,525 --> 00:57:48,428
[SPEAKER_00]: That was resolved in March, where the town upheld the bylaw by a margin of 10%, 55 to 45%.

603
00:57:50,129 --> 00:57:56,951
[SPEAKER_00]: So at this point, it turns out there are a number of people in a number of towns who are watching Lexington to see what would happen.

604
00:57:56,971 --> 00:58:03,814
[SPEAKER_00]: I think sensibly enough thinking if this failed in Lexington, they might not want to try it yet in their own towns.

605
00:58:07,073 --> 00:58:14,875
[SPEAKER_00]: a vote at town meeting in Arlington, either tonight or Wednesday, depending on how quickly they move on a similar proposal that has exactly the same phase out.

606
00:58:15,775 --> 00:58:21,736
[SPEAKER_00]: There will be a vote in Brookline on a phase out at the end of May or early June, depending on how quickly they move.

607
00:58:21,776 --> 00:58:23,356
[SPEAKER_00]: And there may be one in Belmont.

608
00:58:24,416 --> 00:58:36,839
[SPEAKER_00]: And there are a number of other towns like Winchester, where people have motions in play to impose less extreme restrictions, you know, with no phase out, but with seasonal limitations and hourly limitations and so on.

609
00:58:37,766 --> 00:58:38,386
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's spreading.

610
00:58:38,426 --> 00:58:40,428
[SPEAKER_00]: I was actually looking, I'm at the wrong computer.

611
00:58:40,468 --> 00:58:44,130
[SPEAKER_00]: I have a map of where these things have been proposed or enacted.

612
00:58:44,190 --> 00:58:49,193
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's a large chunk of the Boston area now.

613
00:58:49,634 --> 00:58:55,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Boston has hearings on a bill that would be actually more extreme than some of the town ones.

614
00:58:57,118 --> 00:58:58,699
[SPEAKER_00]: So anyway, that's where things stand.

615
00:58:59,160 --> 00:59:03,883
[SPEAKER_00]: I think we'll know more within two months about whether this is,

616
00:59:05,215 --> 00:59:10,097
[SPEAKER_00]: you know, a one-off in Lexington or whether it's the beginning of a trend.

617
00:59:10,817 --> 00:59:12,817
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just give you one other piece of background.

618
00:59:14,918 --> 00:59:23,181
[SPEAKER_00]: When Washington DC passed this about three years ago, their ban actually went into, the phase-out is over, the ban went into effect this calendar year.

619
00:59:24,701 --> 00:59:30,423
[SPEAKER_00]: It did start a trickle of similar laws in surrounding towns.

620
00:59:31,560 --> 00:59:39,104
[SPEAKER_00]: So we'll see, I think, in another month or two, whether that's the case here, whether Lexington is, you know, a fluke.

621
00:59:40,225 --> 00:59:41,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Anyway, that's the short story.

622
00:59:41,605 --> 00:59:44,847
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have other questions, of course, I'd be happy to answer them.

623
00:59:48,789 --> 00:59:51,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Go ahead, anyone else?

624
00:59:52,391 --> 00:59:53,972
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, Gaston, you had a question.

625
00:59:57,654 --> 00:59:58,454
[SPEAKER_00]: You're muted, Gaston.

626
01:00:01,472 --> 01:00:02,853
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought you're muted.

627
01:00:04,515 --> 01:00:05,075
[SPEAKER_04]: Sorry about that.

628
01:00:05,536 --> 01:00:07,498
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, no, thank you for the presentation was very interesting.

629
01:00:07,518 --> 01:00:08,518
[SPEAKER_04]: I have tons of questions.

630
01:00:08,739 --> 01:00:16,926
[SPEAKER_04]: Um, so the first one is, um, why only leaf blowers and not other?

631
01:00:17,527 --> 01:00:21,050
[SPEAKER_04]: Um, I mean, I don't grow grass, so I'm not very familiar with them.

632
01:00:21,090 --> 01:00:26,575
[SPEAKER_04]: But I know about lawnmowers, like the streamers and things like that, when my neighbors do that.

633
01:00:27,055 --> 01:00:31,598
[SPEAKER_04]: is extremely noisy, smelly, and kind of the same things are extreme with leaf blowers.

634
01:00:32,219 --> 01:00:38,363
[SPEAKER_04]: So why just leaf blowers and not other lawn tools, machinery?

635
01:00:38,703 --> 01:00:43,587
[SPEAKER_00]: There have been places that have passed much more, much broader rules.

636
01:00:43,647 --> 01:00:55,455
[SPEAKER_00]: And in fact, the state of California has passed a law that is still waiting for regulatory action that would ban the sale of all gas-powered lawn scaping equipment statewide.

637
01:00:57,162 --> 01:00:59,164
[SPEAKER_00]: We went after leaf blowers for two reasons.

638
01:01:00,985 --> 01:01:06,608
[SPEAKER_00]: The primary one, and there's a legal reason why this was the primary one, is simply the noise.

639
01:01:06,668 --> 01:01:08,249
[SPEAKER_00]: They are extraordinarily noisy.

640
01:01:08,269 --> 01:01:17,254
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the commercial grade leaf blower, a single one, and in our town, we often see four or five at a time on a lot.

641
01:01:17,914 --> 01:01:20,715
[SPEAKER_00]: A single one is 100 decibels at the ear of the operator.

642
01:01:21,616 --> 01:01:22,997
[SPEAKER_00]: To give you an idea how bad that is,

643
01:01:24,905 --> 01:01:31,389
[SPEAKER_00]: National Institute on Occupational Safety and Health sets a recommended exposure limit of 15 minutes per eight hour day.

644
01:01:31,409 --> 01:01:33,971
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's extraordinarily noisy.

645
01:01:34,671 --> 01:01:42,316
[SPEAKER_00]: When you get a bunch of them together, I've measured 93 decibels in my neighborhood at a property line on several occasions.

646
01:01:42,456 --> 01:01:50,481
[SPEAKER_00]: And to give you an idea what that means, a low altitude flyover of an F-35 is 94 decibels.

647
01:01:50,761 --> 01:01:52,242
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's extraordinarily noisy.

648
01:01:53,226 --> 01:01:54,407
[SPEAKER_00]: So that was the primary reason.

649
01:01:54,447 --> 01:02:06,916
[SPEAKER_00]: The other reason that had a big impact, I think, on the town vote, but it was not central politically, and I'll tell you why, is that there are very few pieces of equipment that use two-stroke engines.

650
01:02:08,257 --> 01:02:13,241
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically virtually all leaf blowers, chainsaws, and some string trimmers.

651
01:02:14,081 --> 01:02:17,544
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason they use them is that the power to weight ratio is very high.

652
01:02:18,758 --> 01:02:21,420
[SPEAKER_00]: but they are extraordinarily dirty machines.

653
01:02:22,121 --> 01:02:25,584
[SPEAKER_00]: It's hard to overestimate or overstate how dirty they are.

654
01:02:27,465 --> 01:02:42,057
[SPEAKER_00]: California Resources Board estimated that using one, a gas leaf blower for an hour, which is about a third of the typical usage to clear a lawn in Lexington, generates as much ozone producing emissions as driving a Toyota Camry 1,100 miles.

655
01:02:44,387 --> 01:02:48,670
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's worse than that because the stuff that's emitted is highly toxic.

656
01:02:48,750 --> 01:02:52,353
[SPEAKER_00]: It's PM 2.5, extremely small particulate emissions.

657
01:02:53,254 --> 01:02:56,056
[SPEAKER_00]: A lot of volatile organic compounds that are highly toxic.

658
01:02:56,096 --> 01:02:58,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Some are carcinogens like benzene.

659
01:02:58,818 --> 01:03:00,400
[SPEAKER_00]: They're extraordinarily dangerous.

660
01:03:02,141 --> 01:03:11,028
[SPEAKER_00]: That wasn't as central politically because there's some argument about whether towns have the prerogative to impose environmental restrictions on their own.

661
01:03:12,450 --> 01:03:18,152
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think it had a big influence on how people voted, but in terms of our proposal, it was secondary.

662
01:03:19,693 --> 01:03:23,774
[SPEAKER_00]: It was, however, I think this made a big difference politically.

663
01:03:25,415 --> 01:03:32,517
[SPEAKER_00]: It was the reason why, or one of two reasons why, our Board of Health voted unanimously to support this bylaw.

664
01:03:34,005 --> 01:03:47,231
[SPEAKER_00]: and they were unwilling to take a stand about residents because they said there just aren't good data about exposure levels, but they issued a statement saying that both the emissions and the noise pose a clear health risk for workers.

665
01:03:49,432 --> 01:04:02,338
[SPEAKER_00]: So we figured we'll take on the one that is, in a sense, I don't wanna say politically easier because it was not politically easy, but it was less politically difficult than dealing with lawnmowers, for example.

666
01:04:03,922 --> 01:04:09,225
[SPEAKER_00]: And it was the most offensive of the machines that are currently used and the most dangerous of the machines that were used.

667
01:04:12,406 --> 01:04:21,830
[SPEAKER_00]: We did, I should say, there was a misunderstanding about an earlier version of the motion that led people to think that it would ban lawnmowers.

668
01:04:21,990 --> 01:04:24,071
[SPEAKER_00]: And there was a firestorm of protests.

669
01:04:24,551 --> 01:04:26,852
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it really generated an enormous fuss.

670
01:04:31,714 --> 01:04:32,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Dan, this is Bob Payne.

671
01:04:32,975 --> 01:04:42,620
[SPEAKER_01]: What are the arguments on the side of the operators or the commercial vendors to oppose such an ordinance?

672
01:04:43,401 --> 01:04:46,062
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they had several and some were valid.

673
01:04:47,703 --> 01:04:48,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Some, I think, were not.

674
01:04:48,623 --> 01:04:51,505
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, for instance, one of the big things that they pushed toward the end was that

675
01:04:52,666 --> 01:04:58,229
[SPEAKER_00]: They pointed out accurately enough that lithium mining is in most cases environmentally destructive.

676
01:04:58,289 --> 01:05:01,190
[SPEAKER_00]: But I mean, the extraction of petroleum is environmentally destructive.

677
01:05:01,210 --> 01:05:03,031
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not really the issue.

678
01:05:04,012 --> 01:05:09,134
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's a serious disruption to their business for two reasons.

679
01:05:09,695 --> 01:05:12,196
[SPEAKER_00]: One is that the electric machines are less powerful.

680
01:05:13,717 --> 01:05:16,658
[SPEAKER_00]: And they would argue that it's not feasible to use them.

681
01:05:16,678 --> 01:05:17,779
[SPEAKER_00]: That's simply untrue.

682
01:05:19,205 --> 01:05:26,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Landscape companies that have been using electric blowers for a decade and making a profit, but you have to work differently because machines are less powerful.

683
01:05:27,047 --> 01:05:40,669
[SPEAKER_00]: So for example, the person who does my yard, who has never owned it, it's been in business eight years and has never owned a gas blower, said that a large lot, his crews bring the leaves into smaller piles and collect from that rather than blowing across an entire property.

684
01:05:40,709 --> 01:05:43,270
[SPEAKER_00]: So it means a change in how they work.

685
01:05:44,390 --> 01:05:48,811
[SPEAKER_00]: It does mean for large, heavily treed properties, an increase in costs.

686
01:05:49,815 --> 01:06:00,020
[SPEAKER_00]: The best estimates we were able to get from people with experience is that it's not uncommon for people with large properties to have a 10 or 20% increase in the cost of clearing leaves.

687
01:06:01,021 --> 01:06:08,565
[SPEAKER_00]: The landscapers here claimed that it would triple costs, but the landscapers who were actually doing it with electric equipment said that's absolute garbage, it's nonsense.

688
01:06:09,625 --> 01:06:18,910
[SPEAKER_00]: The other argument which really carried a lot of weight is that when you use gas leaf blowers, you spread costs fairly evenly over time.

689
01:06:19,797 --> 01:06:21,557
[SPEAKER_00]: The blowers are not terribly expensive.

690
01:06:22,497 --> 01:06:27,258
[SPEAKER_00]: The maintenance is fairly expensive and the fuel is expensive, but that's all spread out over time.

691
01:06:28,779 --> 01:06:33,099
[SPEAKER_00]: The economic model with electric lawn equipment is very different.

692
01:06:33,139 --> 01:06:46,842
[SPEAKER_00]: You pay a lot up front, particularly because of batteries, and you pay much less over time because the energy costs are lower and there's virtually no maintenance, except in unusual circumstances.

693
01:06:47,873 --> 01:06:50,837
[SPEAKER_00]: So they face a substantial upfront cost.

694
01:06:54,081 --> 01:07:01,270
[SPEAKER_00]: They often presented that as if there were, in fact, they invariably presented it as if there were no offsetting long-term costs, savings.

695
01:07:01,831 --> 01:07:04,254
[SPEAKER_00]: They would simply say, oh, look, we have to spend all this money for batteries.

696
01:07:05,115 --> 01:07:06,617
[SPEAKER_00]: And they would simply neglect to mention

697
01:07:07,758 --> 01:07:12,180
[SPEAKER_00]: that their costs over three or four or five years were actually fairly similar.

698
01:07:12,700 --> 01:07:14,141
[SPEAKER_00]: So we went to an organization.

699
01:07:14,221 --> 01:07:16,002
[SPEAKER_00]: If you embark on this, you may find them useful.

700
01:07:16,022 --> 01:07:31,849
[SPEAKER_00]: There's an organization, it's a for-profit company called AGZA, the American Green Zone Alliance, which makes its money helping jurisdictions and institutions like universities convert from gas to electric landscaping equipment, not just leaf blowers.

701
01:07:32,589 --> 01:07:37,531
[SPEAKER_00]: It's run by a guy who started his career running a gas-powered leaf landscaping company.

702
01:07:39,849 --> 01:07:51,056
[SPEAKER_00]: They have a lot of data that's from California, which we were unwilling to use because the leaf loads are much lighter in California, but they more recently have been generating data in the northern Midwest where leaf loads are heavy.

703
01:07:52,157 --> 01:08:02,804
[SPEAKER_00]: So we persuaded them to give us estimates of four year costs of transitioning a three blower crew.

704
01:08:05,602 --> 01:08:06,963
[SPEAKER_00]: They gave us quite a range.

705
01:08:07,023 --> 01:08:11,046
[SPEAKER_00]: It depends on how much you use them, how often you replace them, what kind of oil you use.

706
01:08:11,146 --> 01:08:12,307
[SPEAKER_00]: That actually makes a big difference.

707
01:08:13,288 --> 01:08:21,254
[SPEAKER_00]: But their estimate of the four-year cost for crew ranged from $0 to $1,250 a year.

708
01:08:22,835 --> 01:08:30,281
[SPEAKER_00]: And the $1,250 sounds like a lot, but if you divide that by the 500 or 600 jobs a crew does over the course of a cleanup, it's $2 or $3 a job.

709
01:08:33,203 --> 01:08:41,949
[SPEAKER_00]: So in terms of operating expenses long-term, it's not a big deal, but it does mean they have to lay out money up front to buy the equipment.

710
01:08:41,989 --> 01:08:43,990
[SPEAKER_00]: So our response to that was two things.

711
01:08:44,050 --> 01:08:49,374
[SPEAKER_00]: First, we said, tell us, we said to landscapers, tell us how long you actually use the equipment.

712
01:08:50,735 --> 01:08:55,238
[SPEAKER_00]: And it varies, but most of them keep lowers two to four years, right?

713
01:08:55,298 --> 01:08:57,359
[SPEAKER_00]: So we said, let's make a three and a half year transition.

714
01:08:57,839 --> 01:09:01,622
[SPEAKER_00]: You can gradually buy these machines as your old machines are run down.

715
01:09:02,512 --> 01:09:07,254
[SPEAKER_00]: There were a lot of residents who were very displeased, who said, I don't want to listen to this stuff for three and a half more years.

716
01:09:07,874 --> 01:09:09,055
[SPEAKER_00]: But that was our compromise.

717
01:09:09,775 --> 01:09:12,576
[SPEAKER_00]: There are also efforts underway to cushion it.

718
01:09:12,776 --> 01:09:25,421
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a bill before the House in Beacon Hill that would let towns set up programs to provide support for the initial purchase.

719
01:09:26,762 --> 01:09:28,123
[SPEAKER_00]: There's actually an effort by

720
01:09:29,785 --> 01:09:34,046
[SPEAKER_00]: Catherine's Clark's office to get an appropriation for that for her district.

721
01:09:35,046 --> 01:09:41,208
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is a lot of interest in, and I think this is what will probably fly in long-term, bulk purchasing arrangements.

722
01:09:44,689 --> 01:09:53,231
[SPEAKER_00]: When we started calling, my committee started calling commercial vendors and just off the top, they would say, all right, we'll give you 15%.

723
01:09:54,692 --> 01:09:59,173
[SPEAKER_00]: But I think if you got three or four towns together, which would be three towns would probably be

724
01:10:00,493 --> 01:10:07,498
[SPEAKER_00]: 60, 70 landscapers, you could probably negotiate a really substantial bulk discount.

725
01:10:07,618 --> 01:10:10,099
[SPEAKER_00]: But for now, anyway, those are the big arguments.

726
01:10:10,179 --> 01:10:16,223
[SPEAKER_00]: The total costs, which they exaggerated, the initial costs, which I think were a real concern.

727
01:10:18,384 --> 01:10:27,070
[SPEAKER_00]: They also made a strong pitch in Lexington, which I think was a mistake on their part to cast this as an issue of property rights.

728
01:10:28,897 --> 01:10:32,600
[SPEAKER_00]: And the immediate response to that was, what about the rights of the property owners next door?

729
01:10:33,921 --> 01:10:36,163
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, who really don't want to listen to 90 decibels.

730
01:10:37,945 --> 01:10:39,846
[SPEAKER_00]: But I think that was the big, big arguments were.

731
01:10:39,926 --> 01:10:46,752
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you go ahead, I have a pile of their materials, which I'd be happy to send on if you decide to go ahead.

732
01:10:46,772 --> 01:10:51,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Because from what the people in Arlington have told me, it's exactly the same arguments there that we had.

733
01:10:54,999 --> 01:10:56,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Barry, you have your hand up.

734
01:11:00,292 --> 01:11:02,713
[SPEAKER_01]: And yes, so I think Luke had his up before.

735
01:11:02,833 --> 01:11:03,193
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sorry.

736
01:11:03,213 --> 01:11:03,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

737
01:11:03,633 --> 01:11:04,113
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sorry.

738
01:11:04,733 --> 01:11:05,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Luke, you can go ahead.

739
01:11:05,514 --> 01:11:05,954
[SPEAKER_08]: Thank you.

740
01:11:06,234 --> 01:11:06,974
[SPEAKER_08]: No, that's fine.

741
01:11:07,074 --> 01:11:07,454
[SPEAKER_08]: Thank you.

742
01:11:07,974 --> 01:11:09,855
[SPEAKER_08]: Um, so I, forgive me.

743
01:11:09,875 --> 01:11:10,915
[SPEAKER_08]: I have two questions.

744
01:11:11,095 --> 01:11:17,117
[SPEAKER_08]: One is, uh, uh, is the ban a gas ban or is it a two stroke engine ban?

745
01:11:17,137 --> 01:11:27,580
[SPEAKER_08]: Um, so in the end are, uh, is it requiring an electric system or is it requiring just a quieter system?

746
01:11:28,579 --> 01:11:39,067
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a superb question because a lot of jurisdictions have tried not banning gas leaf blowers entirely and trying to control what kind of leaf blowers are used.

747
01:11:40,869 --> 01:11:45,532
[SPEAKER_00]: Our conversations with the people in jurisdictions that have tried that suggest that it just doesn't work.

748
01:11:46,973 --> 01:11:55,140
[SPEAKER_00]: First of all, the four-stroke gas-powered leaf blowers tend to be less powerful because of the unfavorable power to weight ratio.

749
01:11:55,180 --> 01:11:56,401
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's not what they want anyway.

750
01:11:57,270 --> 01:12:08,575
[SPEAKER_00]: What a lot of towns around here have done, Newton has done at Cambridge, I think Brookline, is to impose a 65 decibel sound limit.

751
01:12:09,115 --> 01:12:15,417
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem with that is you have to have somebody go and measure it, right?

752
01:12:15,497 --> 01:12:26,342
[SPEAKER_00]: And so what some landscapers admitted, frankly, is that when they're working in Cambridge, if they see somebody they're worried about, they throttle down for a minute, and then when they leave, they just go back to work.

753
01:12:27,023 --> 01:12:35,851
[SPEAKER_00]: But our thing about this was really shaped a lot by our conversation with our chief of police, who in this town is the person who's going to have to enforce this.

754
01:12:37,252 --> 01:12:38,954
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, it's got to be simple.

755
01:12:40,015 --> 01:12:46,500
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, if I get a call that somebody's doing house construction at 630 in the morning, which is illegal, it's simple.

756
01:12:46,561 --> 01:12:47,581
[SPEAKER_00]: I send somebody out.

757
01:12:47,641 --> 01:12:48,302
[SPEAKER_00]: They say, it's 630.

758
01:12:48,382 --> 01:12:49,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Here's your ticket.

759
01:12:50,517 --> 01:12:52,599
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, if you can make it that simple, we can do it.

760
01:12:52,639 --> 01:12:57,884
[SPEAKER_00]: But he said, they're not going to go out with anti-certified meters and measure 50 feet and so on.

761
01:12:59,085 --> 01:13:05,570
[SPEAKER_00]: So we decided the only thing that made it enforceable is to just ban them entirely.

762
01:13:06,271 --> 01:13:13,017
[SPEAKER_00]: We spoke to people in some towns that said they actually no longer try to enforce the other limits because it just is such a headache.

763
01:13:13,740 --> 01:13:14,601
[SPEAKER_08]: But banning what?

764
01:13:14,681 --> 01:13:16,822
[SPEAKER_08]: Banning leaf blowers, period, or?

765
01:13:16,902 --> 01:13:19,844
[SPEAKER_00]: We just ban all gas-powered leaf blowers.

766
01:13:20,224 --> 01:13:21,224
[SPEAKER_00]: You can still use electric.

767
01:13:21,845 --> 01:13:26,888
[SPEAKER_00]: You will face, if you do this, by the way, a small but vocal group of people who want all blowers banned.

768
01:13:27,248 --> 01:13:27,488
[SPEAKER_08]: Yeah.

769
01:13:28,729 --> 01:13:29,809
[SPEAKER_00]: I can imagine.

770
01:13:30,250 --> 01:13:33,672
[SPEAKER_08]: Electric blowers also make plenty of noise, too.

771
01:13:33,692 --> 01:13:41,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they make a huge amount less, but they do damage overwintering pollinators, which became an issue here.

772
01:13:43,628 --> 01:13:53,214
[SPEAKER_00]: What I said to the other people in the, we had some people in our referendum campaign who were concerned about that, some who weren't.

773
01:13:53,274 --> 01:13:55,375
[SPEAKER_00]: And what I suggested is forget about it.

774
01:13:56,095 --> 01:14:01,839
[SPEAKER_00]: I actually am concerned about it, but we're not gonna win this referendum if we tell people they can't use any blowers.

775
01:14:01,939 --> 01:14:04,040
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just not politically feasible now.

776
01:14:05,001 --> 01:14:07,022
[SPEAKER_00]: But in terms of noise, there's a huge difference.

777
01:14:07,902 --> 01:14:09,283
[SPEAKER_00]: We created some charts.

778
01:14:09,303 --> 01:14:13,625
[SPEAKER_00]: We had the advantage of having an acoustical consultant from MIT working with us.

779
01:14:14,266 --> 01:14:16,167
[SPEAKER_00]: So we were able to generate numbers pretty quickly.

780
01:14:16,207 --> 01:14:31,095
[SPEAKER_00]: But one of our charts, which he created, compared four commercial grade electric leaf blower battery powered backpack leaf blowers to one standard commercial grade gas leaf power blower.

781
01:14:31,175 --> 01:14:36,318
[SPEAKER_00]: And the answer is the single gas leaf powered blower generated twice the noise of four electric blowers.

782
01:14:37,430 --> 01:14:48,522
[SPEAKER_00]: But the other thing, and tell me if I'm getting into things that are too technical, all of these other numbers you will encounter use what is called the A-weighted decibel scale.

783
01:14:49,183 --> 01:14:53,608
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is a scale that's weighted to emphasize the frequencies that are most noticeable in human hearing.

784
01:14:55,129 --> 01:14:58,473
[SPEAKER_00]: What that scale does not emphasize is low frequencies.

785
01:14:59,958 --> 01:15:08,240
[SPEAKER_00]: And the real difference apart from overall noise levels between gas-powered leaf blowers and electric blowers is gas-powered leaf blowers produce a lot of low-frequency noise.

786
01:15:09,440 --> 01:15:11,140
[SPEAKER_00]: And the impact of that is twofold.

787
01:15:11,260 --> 01:15:16,121
[SPEAKER_00]: One is it travels much further, and the other is it penetrates surfaces much better.

788
01:15:17,102 --> 01:15:23,943
[SPEAKER_00]: So when you have somebody two doors down from you doing this and you close the windows and it's still annoying, that's why it's the low-frequency noise.

789
01:15:25,199 --> 01:15:32,324
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I mean, our acoustical experts said he thought all of the numbers we were using understated the problem because they were using the wrong scale.

790
01:15:34,566 --> 01:15:40,130
[SPEAKER_08]: Well, and you answered in part my second question, but I do just want to get it out there.

791
01:15:40,210 --> 01:15:41,851
[SPEAKER_08]: And that is in terms of enforcement.

792
01:15:42,792 --> 01:15:51,038
[SPEAKER_08]: So this will be enforced by the police department and basically a neighbor can report and see what.

793
01:15:51,518 --> 01:15:51,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

794
01:15:53,779 --> 01:15:56,481
[SPEAKER_00]: The other towns that have done this have done all sorts of things.

795
01:15:56,541 --> 01:16:03,246
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, in our case, it's partly because we're small enough that we don't have some of the agencies that, say, Cambridge has.

796
01:16:05,508 --> 01:16:11,373
[SPEAKER_00]: So legally, we have four agencies that have authority to enforce noise ordinances.

797
01:16:11,473 --> 01:16:19,739
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the building commissioner, who's clearly not going to do this, the fire department, which is clearly not going to do this, the police, and the health department.

798
01:16:20,980 --> 01:16:23,362
[SPEAKER_00]: So in practice, what this boils down to

799
01:16:24,258 --> 01:16:28,640
[SPEAKER_00]: is that when there is persistent noise, that can be measured.

800
01:16:29,781 --> 01:16:45,927
[SPEAKER_00]: So from ongoing breaking of rock and construction, for example, the health department takes it because they actually have staff who can go out with ANSI certified meters and take the measurements that are required under state law for the standard state standard, which is a 10 decibel increment.

801
01:16:46,568 --> 01:16:50,589
[SPEAKER_00]: But they have no way of going out there when the landscaper is next door to me for 45 minutes.

802
01:16:52,713 --> 01:16:53,674
[SPEAKER_00]: It had to be the police.

803
01:16:55,935 --> 01:16:56,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you.

804
01:16:57,255 --> 01:16:58,096
[SPEAKER_01]: OK, let's see.

805
01:16:58,116 --> 01:16:59,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's go to Barry.

806
01:16:59,256 --> 01:16:59,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Your questions.

807
01:17:01,157 --> 01:17:02,898
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, mine's simpler.

808
01:17:05,439 --> 01:17:07,540
[SPEAKER_03]: Can you attach numbers to the cost?

809
01:17:07,820 --> 01:17:14,324
[SPEAKER_03]: What does a typical electric leaf blower cost up front?

810
01:17:15,459 --> 01:17:29,748
[SPEAKER_03]: And how does that compare to, say, monthly revenues for a single crew, which I imagine a crew would have one leaf blower, right?

811
01:17:30,209 --> 01:17:30,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

812
01:17:30,789 --> 01:17:33,150
[SPEAKER_00]: Around here, the crews often have three to five.

813
01:17:33,891 --> 01:17:37,793
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, so there's a whole bunch of people in the crew, and each one has a leaf blower.

814
01:17:37,974 --> 01:17:40,375
[SPEAKER_00]: So I don't have those numbers off the top of my head.

815
01:17:40,475 --> 01:17:42,396
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd be happy to get them to you.

816
01:17:42,537 --> 01:17:44,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Email me, and I'll send you a page that has them.

817
01:17:48,498 --> 01:17:51,080
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a low entry barrier field, right?

818
01:17:51,180 --> 01:17:55,344
[SPEAKER_00]: So the way most of this works is people go in and finance equipment.

819
01:17:55,484 --> 01:17:57,867
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, leaf blowers are not their expensive equipment.

820
01:17:57,887 --> 01:18:00,929
[SPEAKER_00]: The expensive equipment are their trucks, their trailers, right?

821
01:18:02,971 --> 01:18:06,114
[SPEAKER_00]: So would some of them have to finance purchases?

822
01:18:06,194 --> 01:18:06,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Probably.

823
01:18:07,392 --> 01:18:07,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Probably so.

824
01:18:08,012 --> 01:18:10,034
[SPEAKER_03]: OK, that's what I'm getting at.

825
01:18:10,054 --> 01:18:23,442
[SPEAKER_03]: It just seems like, you know, amounts of money that might sound like it's an expensive piece of equipment compared to the overall cost of running the business might be inconsequential or it might not be.

826
01:18:23,602 --> 01:18:25,263
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, that's why I'm asking for the numbers.

827
01:18:25,783 --> 01:18:27,064
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it actually is.

828
01:18:27,624 --> 01:18:30,646
[SPEAKER_00]: The real cost of of clearing leaves are two things.

829
01:18:30,706 --> 01:18:33,728
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the great bulk of the cost is labor.

830
01:18:35,108 --> 01:18:35,729
[SPEAKER_00]: And tipping fees.

831
01:18:36,704 --> 01:18:39,627
[SPEAKER_00]: In most of this area, it costs a lot of money to dump the leaves.

832
01:18:40,988 --> 01:18:49,915
[SPEAKER_00]: And the equipment costs, even the expensive equipment costs, when you average it over lots and lots of jobs, things like big trucks, are not what most of the bill is.

833
01:18:50,456 --> 01:18:57,321
[SPEAKER_00]: There was a landscaper in Lexington, he actually quit Lexington when this bill passed, who used to provide bills that broke it down.

834
01:18:58,462 --> 01:19:03,667
[SPEAKER_00]: And one of our select board members used his service, and close to half of the bill was tipping fees.

835
01:19:04,963 --> 01:19:11,508
[SPEAKER_00]: So this is not a major portion of their expense, but it is a major outlay initially.

836
01:19:11,648 --> 01:19:15,010
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, and it's not, again, it's not the blowers for the most part.

837
01:19:15,630 --> 01:19:17,852
[SPEAKER_00]: There's one company where that's an exception.

838
01:19:17,912 --> 01:19:18,633
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the batteries.

839
01:19:19,994 --> 01:19:22,455
[SPEAKER_00]: You need a lot of batteries to get through a day.

840
01:19:22,495 --> 01:19:33,323
[SPEAKER_00]: So the most experienced electric landscaper in this area, this guy in Concord, who said he sends out a crew with three blowers

841
01:19:34,278 --> 01:19:35,319
[SPEAKER_00]: with 24 batteries.

842
01:19:37,740 --> 01:19:46,704
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said when he converted, when he purchased the equipment, he said the bulk of his expense, other than things like trucks, was batteries, wasn't chargers.

843
01:19:47,024 --> 01:19:48,625
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't blowers, it was batteries.

844
01:19:49,605 --> 01:20:03,352
[SPEAKER_00]: There are some people, not here, but in the Washington DC area, there are some landscapers who handle this differently and put in their contracts with customers that they can charge batteries at a customer's house for the next job.

845
01:20:04,633 --> 01:20:06,374
[SPEAKER_00]: which costs the customer virtually nothing.

846
01:20:07,975 --> 01:20:13,559
[SPEAKER_00]: So I asked George Kuret, the fellow here, why he doesn't do that.

847
01:20:14,500 --> 01:20:16,341
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, I don't want my crews encumbered.

848
01:20:16,401 --> 01:20:17,822
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, I'd rather lay the money out.

849
01:20:17,842 --> 01:20:19,043
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to worry about weather.

850
01:20:19,163 --> 01:20:21,724
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to worry about going to house that doesn't have outdoor outlets.

851
01:20:22,305 --> 01:20:25,307
[SPEAKER_00]: I just know when I send the crew out, they can work all day and come back.

852
01:20:27,208 --> 01:20:31,911
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's the, you know, when you're buying eight batteries per blower, that's the money.

853
01:20:33,813 --> 01:20:37,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Somebody asked about whether residents are fined.

854
01:20:39,857 --> 01:20:47,741
[SPEAKER_00]: That portion of our bylaw was written by our town council, not by us, but us being the committee, but we actually agreed with him.

855
01:20:50,743 --> 01:20:55,526
[SPEAKER_00]: The punishments can be applied to either the contractor or the resident.

856
01:20:56,787 --> 01:21:02,410
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was done partly to be consistent with other parts of our town bylaw, but it was done partly because

857
01:21:03,529 --> 01:21:06,931
[SPEAKER_00]: some of the landscapers, not the big established companies, would be hard to find.

858
01:21:07,271 --> 01:21:12,274
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, three guys in a truck come up, they do the job, they're gone, and you get there and it's too late.

859
01:21:13,435 --> 01:21:21,380
[SPEAKER_00]: So the idea was that if it's impossible to find the landscaper, you can find the homeowner who will then stop hiring a landscaper who does that.

860
01:21:22,081 --> 01:21:23,601
[SPEAKER_00]: The fines aren't terribly large.

861
01:21:25,323 --> 01:21:29,705
[SPEAKER_00]: I think ours are, I think they top out after repeated violations at $200, but

862
01:21:31,212 --> 01:21:36,515
[SPEAKER_00]: when you're charging somebody $600 to clean the yard, a $200 fine is a real disincentive.

863
01:21:39,216 --> 01:21:42,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's see, we have just a few minutes to, with Dan.

864
01:21:42,858 --> 01:21:49,861
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, I have a few points still discussed by someone else that hasn't asked yet, wants to ask something, I'm happy to let them go first.

865
01:21:52,003 --> 01:21:54,285
[SPEAKER_01]: I think, go ahead and take another two.

866
01:21:54,645 --> 01:21:57,327
[SPEAKER_09]: I had one question for Dan.

867
01:21:58,988 --> 01:22:04,812
[SPEAKER_09]: One of the things that I haven't, like you really have been focusing on the leaf blowers to blow leaves.

868
01:22:05,573 --> 01:22:16,361
[SPEAKER_09]: And I feel like here in Medford, sorry my video keeps cutting out, we have a lot of people who seem to use leaf blowers to move a little bit of

869
01:22:17,061 --> 01:22:19,802
[SPEAKER_09]: grass clippings off the sidewalk.

870
01:22:20,122 --> 01:22:23,604
[SPEAKER_09]: Has anybody said, what about using brooms?

871
01:22:23,684 --> 01:22:25,545
[SPEAKER_09]: And we have much smaller yards.

872
01:22:26,025 --> 01:22:27,866
[SPEAKER_09]: Nobody has an acre in Medford.

873
01:22:29,366 --> 01:22:32,928
[SPEAKER_09]: I don't think there are any private properties that large.

874
01:22:33,188 --> 01:22:40,972
[SPEAKER_09]: They're probably industrial or business properties, but no homeowners on that site.

875
01:22:41,012 --> 01:22:46,234
[SPEAKER_03]: I've seen leaf blowers used to blow construction waste, which is like,

876
01:22:48,873 --> 01:22:52,354
[SPEAKER_09]: I guess, did any of that come up in your discussion?

877
01:22:52,374 --> 01:22:55,816
[SPEAKER_09]: It's sort of inappropriate use of- Absolutely.

878
01:22:55,936 --> 01:23:02,038
[SPEAKER_00]: And by the way, I saw people using it rather than brooms to get rid of sand from brickwork.

879
01:23:03,399 --> 01:23:10,682
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the rationale behind the seasonal restrictions, basically saying, because the landscapers, it's actually kind of interesting.

880
01:23:10,762 --> 01:23:14,724
[SPEAKER_00]: The landscapers here really did not fight seasonal restrictions.

881
01:23:14,764 --> 01:23:17,965
[SPEAKER_00]: They argued about what they should be, what the terminal date should be.

882
01:23:19,050 --> 01:23:26,894
[SPEAKER_00]: But some of them in our public meetings, as he did in those meetings, were actually said, you know, we don't really need these things outside of fall and spring cleanup.

883
01:23:28,695 --> 01:23:43,103
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's, I think, politically easy to impose a seasonal restriction where basically you let them use it where it's most helpful to them, which is really fall cleanup, and ban it in the summer, which is what Newton has done, for example.

884
01:23:43,563 --> 01:23:47,745
[SPEAKER_00]: They ban it only, I think it's Memorial Day to Labor Day, something like that.

885
01:23:50,017 --> 01:23:51,438
[SPEAKER_00]: I think that's politically much easier.

886
01:23:52,318 --> 01:23:55,479
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what chair of our select board wanted me to do.

887
01:23:55,559 --> 01:24:01,601
[SPEAKER_00]: And she referred to, she said that that would be less controversial and it would keep the core of the proposal.

888
01:24:01,661 --> 01:24:03,742
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, no, the core of the problem is the fall.

889
01:24:04,762 --> 01:24:16,846
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when people are most upset because it's, I think Medford is probably not as heavily treated as Lexington, but it's, you know, you have a lot of trees and it's just endless hour after hour after hour.

890
01:24:18,397 --> 01:24:21,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereas in the summer, you know, somebody comes and mows the lawn and they do this for a little bit and they go.

891
01:24:23,199 --> 01:24:25,680
[SPEAKER_00]: So we decided to, you know, bite the bullet and try the whole thing.

892
01:24:25,700 --> 01:24:32,802
[SPEAKER_00]: But I think politically, there are already five or six towns in this area that have banned them outside of fall and spring cleanups.

893
01:24:32,882 --> 01:24:34,903
[SPEAKER_00]: And I just don't think that would be that hard to do.

894
01:24:34,923 --> 01:24:44,707
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I don't understand actually watching them in the summer, why it makes economic sense because they've got these huge blowers

895
01:24:45,622 --> 01:24:47,964
[SPEAKER_00]: And they're blowing stuff back and forth, basically.

896
01:24:49,565 --> 01:24:50,205
[SPEAKER_00]: It'd be quicker.

897
01:24:50,326 --> 01:24:52,247
[SPEAKER_00]: I really think it'd be quicker to take a push broom.

898
01:24:54,188 --> 01:25:02,415
[SPEAKER_00]: But anyway, I think we just decided that we didn't want to stick with that because it wasn't the core of the complaints we got from residents.

899
01:25:02,475 --> 01:25:04,977
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, what residents were really upset about was the fall.

900
01:25:07,998 --> 01:25:10,402
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's see, Dan, I think you have to leave at eight o'clock.

901
01:25:10,803 --> 01:25:11,804
[SPEAKER_00]: I have to leave pretty quickly.

902
01:25:11,824 --> 01:25:13,708
[SPEAKER_00]: I would be happy to come back to another meeting.

903
01:25:13,788 --> 01:25:14,389
[SPEAKER_00]: I apologize.

904
01:25:14,469 --> 01:25:14,669
[SPEAKER_00]: I have.

905
01:25:14,869 --> 01:25:15,791
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all right.

906
01:25:15,811 --> 01:25:19,136
[SPEAKER_00]: I got was emailed that I had this is actually in the middle of something else I have scheduled.

907
01:25:20,601 --> 01:25:23,202
[SPEAKER_00]: But I would be happy to come back when you've thought about this.

908
01:25:24,162 --> 01:25:33,204
[SPEAKER_09]: And I think we might want to try and see if we can get our city council to have you talk with them because they're the ones who are actually discussing the ordinance right now.

909
01:25:33,865 --> 01:25:38,746
[SPEAKER_09]: And I think it would be very helpful to have them hear directly from you.

910
01:25:38,766 --> 01:25:40,206
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd be happy to do that.

911
01:25:40,226 --> 01:25:46,188
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, just, you know, give me, let me know in advance so we can work out scheduling, but I'd be happy to do that.

912
01:25:47,530 --> 01:25:49,370
[SPEAKER_00]: So I am going to have to run, I apologize.

913
01:25:50,511 --> 01:25:51,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much.

914
01:25:51,351 --> 01:25:52,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you very much.

915
01:25:52,491 --> 01:25:54,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Very, very much.

916
01:25:54,632 --> 01:25:59,713
[SPEAKER_00]: I have people waiting on another zoom, so I have to run back to that.

917
01:25:59,733 --> 01:26:05,394
[SPEAKER_04]: The three points I went to make, I think they applied to Medford.

918
01:26:05,414 --> 01:26:05,754
[SPEAKER_04]: So, um, hello?

919
01:26:06,735 --> 01:26:07,035
[SPEAKER_04]: Go ahead.

920
01:26:17,817 --> 01:26:18,157
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, sorry.

921
01:26:18,458 --> 01:26:44,138
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, so the first one is that I'm still not sure that given the size of yards that we have met for compared to Lexington, that it would be, maybe I'm too naive, that it would be too much more of an issue or a pushback to go for a ban on non-human powered

922
01:26:45,103 --> 01:26:48,965
[SPEAKER_04]: leaf moving versus a gas-powered one.

923
01:26:49,465 --> 01:27:03,172
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, I've used a rake in my life, and I'm not positive that I'm a lot slower, if any slower, using a rake to just like collect leaves than what the guys do with the leaf blower.

924
01:27:03,893 --> 01:27:14,418
[SPEAKER_04]: So given the size of our yard in Medford, if we can go for like a ban on non-human powered leaf blowing,

925
01:27:15,724 --> 01:27:19,007
[SPEAKER_04]: I would totally favor it over a ban on gas power.

926
01:27:19,167 --> 01:27:23,591
[SPEAKER_04]: So then we avoid having all these electric issues with the flow.

927
01:27:23,611 --> 01:27:24,472
[SPEAKER_04]: So that's the first one.

928
01:27:24,792 --> 01:27:27,394
[SPEAKER_04]: The second one is that we definitely need a lot of data.

929
01:27:27,594 --> 01:27:39,725
[SPEAKER_04]: So because a lot of people are going to be affected by this, and I heavily support a data-driven approach to all of this, the concerns of the

930
01:27:40,605 --> 01:27:45,187
[SPEAKER_04]: of the, you know, landscaping companies, the resident concerns and stuff.

931
01:27:45,287 --> 01:27:48,668
[SPEAKER_04]: It seems that Dan had, they did actually a great job on collecting data.

932
01:27:48,708 --> 01:27:51,229
[SPEAKER_04]: So that was great to hear.

933
01:27:51,749 --> 01:28:00,893
[SPEAKER_04]: And then the last point is that I also personally agree that the way to do this would be through a referendum.

934
01:28:01,193 --> 01:28:07,095
[SPEAKER_04]: So we need to spend a lot of effort in

935
01:28:07,993 --> 01:28:21,283
[SPEAKER_04]: education and, you know, showing to people how this is bad for health of the workers and of the residents of the ones getting the job done on the neighbors of those getting the job done.

936
01:28:22,784 --> 01:28:32,911
[SPEAKER_04]: Because I just feel that at the end of the day, it is kind of a referendum thing where, you know, let people choose what they want to do.

937
01:28:33,418 --> 01:28:53,043
[SPEAKER_04]: So we do have an education subcommittee, and I think we need to invest a lot of time trying to show, you know, a lot of the stuff that Dan has talked about, and other things to sort of, you know, convince people to try and find out why it is a bad idea to use a leaf blower in the first place.

938
01:28:53,223 --> 01:28:56,463
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, I would just not blow any leaves.

939
01:28:57,344 --> 01:29:03,105
[SPEAKER_04]: And B, in particular, if you're gonna, you know, just move leaves around, use a gas-powered leaf blower.

940
01:29:04,951 --> 01:29:05,171
[SPEAKER_04]: Thank you.

941
01:29:05,912 --> 01:29:07,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you on a comment?

942
01:29:07,592 --> 01:29:11,074
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, I would like to make a motion.

943
01:29:11,555 --> 01:29:13,956
[SPEAKER_03]: I would actually like to make a twin motion.

944
01:29:14,977 --> 01:29:24,602
[SPEAKER_03]: One, I would like to move that the committee endorses a ban or phased out ban on gas powered leaf mowers.

945
01:29:26,315 --> 01:29:41,503
[SPEAKER_03]: And a second and that we as a committee send a letter to the mayor asking for her support on an ordinance to ban or phase out gas powered leaf blowers.

946
01:29:42,923 --> 01:29:43,984
[SPEAKER_07]: I would second that motion.

947
01:29:45,825 --> 01:29:50,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Discussion, maybe we need to have something in writing.

948
01:29:51,347 --> 01:29:52,428
[SPEAKER_01]: Before we actually

949
01:29:55,275 --> 01:29:57,736
[SPEAKER_09]: Do you mean the whole letter or just the idea?

950
01:29:57,756 --> 01:30:00,597
[SPEAKER_09]: Because I was trying to get down, get your motion down.

951
01:30:00,758 --> 01:30:13,644
[SPEAKER_09]: I'd like to, having watched many people take minutes for meetings, I'd like to make sure that I have the motion down correctly and read it back to you before you vote on it, because there was a lot of words in that.

952
01:30:15,184 --> 01:30:16,385
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a good idea, Alicia.

953
01:30:17,027 --> 01:30:26,110
[SPEAKER_09]: that the committee endorse a ban on phased out gas-powered leaf blowers and ask the mayor to support the ban as well?

954
01:30:26,130 --> 01:30:30,912
[SPEAKER_03]: A ban or phase out of gas-powered leaf blowers.

955
01:30:31,092 --> 01:30:32,493
[SPEAKER_09]: Oh, thank you.

956
01:30:32,613 --> 01:30:39,655
[SPEAKER_09]: I typed on instead of or, or a ban or phase out gas-powered leaf blowers.

957
01:30:39,735 --> 01:30:44,657
[SPEAKER_03]: And ask the mayor for her support of such an ordinance to do so.

958
01:30:52,149 --> 01:30:57,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Now in this type of motion is, do we entertain discussions on wording?

959
01:30:59,491 --> 01:31:01,091
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm open to friendly amendment.

960
01:31:03,512 --> 01:31:05,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Anybody, anybody want to.

961
01:31:05,132 --> 01:31:06,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

962
01:31:06,613 --> 01:31:13,435
[SPEAKER_05]: I'd say, you know, a lot of good points over me that all leaf blowers are damaging to the environment.

963
